# Everything here either does not belong in an image or would actively break it.

# Installed inside the image for the image's own platform. Copying the host's
# would ship Windows binaries to a Linux container -- @node-rs/argon2 is a native
# module, and it would fail at require() time, not at build time.
node_modules
**/node_modules

# Built inside the image. A stale host build copied in would silently win over
# the one the image makes.
dist
**/dist
.turbo
**/.turbo

# GENERATED PRISMA CLIENT. Regenerated during the build; copying it in risks
# shipping output from a schema that has since changed.
services/*/src/generated

# SECRETS AND LOCAL STATE. keys/ holds the JWT signing key, which the compose
# setup step generates into a volume instead -- baking one into an image would
# put a private key in every layer anyone can pull.
**/keys

# ENV FILES IN EVERY SHAPE THEY TAKE, not just the exact name `.env`.
#
# `**/.env` matches a file called `.env` and nothing else, and this repository
# does not only have those: the relay carries `.env.ticketing` so one binary can
# be pointed at the other service's outbox. .gitignore already covers it with
# `.env.*`, so it never reaches a commit -- and it went straight into the image,
# carrying RELAY_DATABASE_URL and its credentials. Found by listing what actually
# ended up in /app rather than by reading this file.
#
# So the rule matches the family, and the committed examples are added back:
# they are documentation, they are already public in the repository, and a reader
# poking around inside the image should find them.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example
!**/.env.*.example

.git
.github
*.log

# The web app is built and served on its own (apps/web has its own lockfile and CI job);
# nothing in the backend image runs it.
apps

# TERRAFORM, THE WHOLE DIRECTORY. Nothing in the image runs it, and once anyone has run
# `terraform init` or applied the bootstrap root, it holds what must never be in a layer:
# the bootstrap's local terraform.tfstate, terraform.tfvars with real values, and
# hundreds of megabytes of provider binaries under .terraform/. `COPY . .` would take
# all of it. Excluding the directory covers every name those files take.
infra/terraform

# THE AGENT'S WORKING TREES. .claude/worktrees holds a whole checkout of the repository per
# branch in progress, and `COPY . .` took every one: 100 MB of other branches' source was
# inside eventhub/app:local, found by listing /app in the image (2026-11-14) before its
# first push to ECR. The patterns above happened to keep their keys and .env files out, but
# a worktree's own infra/terraform is not infra/terraform, and a tfvars there would have
# gone in. Nothing in .claude belongs in an image.
.claude
